  "content": {
    "id": "p39-q18-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "18",
    "prompt": "How many times must a die be rolled so that the probability of rolling at least one six is greater than $95\\%$?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$17$ rolls",
      "worked": "Let $n$ be the number of rolls. Using the complement of rolling no sixes,\n$$\\begin{align*}1-\\left(\\frac56\\right)^n&>0.95\\\\\\left(\\frac56\\right)^n&<0.05\\\\n\\log\\left(\\frac56\\right)&<\\log(0.05)\\\\n&>\\frac{\\log(0.05)}{\\log(5/6)}\\\\n&>16.431\\ldots\\end{align*}$$\nThe inequality reverses when dividing by $\\log(\\frac{5}{6})<0$. The minimum number is $17$ rolls.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 29
  },
